A||N||4||MikeSchaefer||Heater affects oil pressure?||||5||
Z||000000||MikeSchaefer||01-27-2001||09:29 PM||||Hi,  I noticed while driving my '84, V-6 today that when the heater is on full blast that my oil pressure gauge drops significantly and the "oil" light comes on.  Is this an actuall drop in pressure or a mis-read by the gauge caused by the change of power in the system?  I checked how much oil was in there and it seems to be ok, not really low or anything.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Michael Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-27-2001||09:44 PM||||Possible when the engine was transplanted into the car the wiring harnesses weren't wired well. The wiring between '84 and later models is very different.<P><BR>Figure I should ask: when the oil light is on, can you hear the lifters ticking or any other odd noises?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-28-2001||01:09 AM||||Actually, it's more like the grounds and power into the gauges suck. Not unusual although the gauges are balanced to have the battery voltage affect them less.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||MikeSchaefer||01-28-2001||04:13 PM||||No unusual sounds or feelings, I'm starting to think you are right about the wiring.  Thanks a lot for your input.<BR>Mike Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-28-2001||06:17 PM||||check all the ground leads to the block and body in the engine bay. there should be several. The main ones to watch out for is the big one that goes from the block to the body, and the small one from the battery to the body.||||reg||1
